  "query_preview": "..\nOvernight, showers and perhaps a rumble of thunder continue as a\nweak disturbance interacts with increasing moisture and elevated\ninstability. Rainfall amounts overnight will remain light, but\nsome localized areas could pick up a quick 0.25\" or so through\nthe morning. Over the past hour, lightning has picked up just\nsouth of the border, with these isolated storms moving into \nsouthern AZ. \n\nTropical Storm Rachel continues to track northeastward toward\nBaja, bringing tropical moisture and dynamic forcing into\nSouthern Arizona. While gradient winds have lessened slightly in\nrecent guidance, strong, gusty winds are still expected. A Wind\nAdvisory remains in place from Tucson westward for Sunday.\nExpect south to southwest winds of 25\u201335 mph with gusts of \n45\u201355+ mph, with localized higher gusts possible near any \nconvective activity. For the Wind Adv east for eastern Pima, \nportions of Santa Cruz, and SE Pinal COs, S/SW sustained winds \nof 25-35 mph, with gusts around 45 mph likely. Once again, \nlocalized wind gusts to 60 mph will be possible due to a threat \nfor isolated strong to severe thunderstorms. \n\nWhile there could be a brief lull from mid morning into early\nafternoon, increasing moisture transported north into AZ from\nRachel's interaction with an upper level trof will increase\ncoverage of showers and storms. \n\nSevere potential exists this afternoon/evening and possibly into\nSunday morning. The main threats remain damaging wind gusts\n(40\u201360 mph), small hail, and localized heavy downpours. Modest\ninstability paired with favorable 0\u20131km/0\u20133km helicity and\nmoderate to strong deep layer shear maintains a non-zero \ntornado threat (2% risk per SPC) primarily across western Pima \nCounty (including Ajo, Lukeville, and Why) from Saturday \nafternoon through early Sunday morning. Looking over some of the\nCAMs, the greatest severe threat would be later in the\nafternoon, the evening, and possibly overnight. There are\nsolutions that bring one or two broken lines of convection\nthrough the western third of the CWA in the E/SE quadrant of \nRachel as she makes landfall in northern Baja. Given the shear, \nsupercells are possible, even as far east as of Tucson. However,\nany tornado threat should remain confined to far western Pima \nand perhaps into central Pima County. \n\nAs for the rain an possible flash flood risk, scattered to \nnumerous showers and storms will pick up this afternoon into \nSunday. Storm total rainfall averages 0.25\" to 0.75\" west of \nTucson and 0.10\" to 0.25\" east of Tucson, with 1 to 2+ inches \npossible in mountain locations or under localized training \ncells. While widespread flooding is not expected, localized \nquick bursts of a half-inch to an inch could yield an isolated \nflash flood threat. Southerly flow ahead of the longwave trough \nkeeps lingering rain/thunderstorm chances (20\u201350%) going Monday \nand Tuesday. Drier air returns by Wednesday, clearing out shower\nactivity across the area. Expect an extended period of below- \nnormal temperatures starting Sunday and persisting through mid- \nweek.\n\nWhile its fairly early, there is an increasing signal for\nanother east wind event late week. Just something to keep on\nyour radar as winds could be fairly strong in the usual easterly\nwind even prone areas.",
